Day 42 – Robotics Math: Path Projection & Cross-Track Error

Today I learned how vector projection can describe a robot's position relative to a planned path.

Key Concepts

Given:

- Start point: A
- Point on planned path: B
- Current robot position: P

1. Planned direction

[
d=B-A
]

This is important when the robot does not start at (0,0).

2. Unit direction vector

[
\hat d=\frac{d}{||d||}
]

This represents the forward direction of the planned path.

3. Robot displacement from start

[
r=P-A
]

4. Distance travelled along the path

[
s=r\cdot\hat d
]

This tells how far the robot has progressed in the planned direction.

5. Closest point on the planned path

Relative to the start:

[
p_{\parallel}=s\hat d
]

Actual world coordinate:

[
C=A+p_{\parallel}
]

6. Off-path error

[
p_{\perp}=P-C
]

Distance from the path:

[
e=||p_{\perp}||
]

This is the robot's cross-track error.

Example Practised

[
A=(-7,-6),\quad B=(4,5),\quad P=(-2,3)
]

Results:

[
d=(11,11)
]

[
\hat d\approx(0.707,0.707)
]

[
r=(5,9)
]

[
s\approx9.90
]

Closest path point:

[
C=(0,1)
]

Off-path vector:

[
P-C=(-2,2)
]

Cross-track error:

[
||P-C||\approx2.83
]

So the robot has progressed approximately 9.90 units along the planned path and is 2.83 units away from it.

Main Understanding

Points tell WHERE an object is.
Vectors tell HOW to move from one point to another.

Memory chain:

[
\boxed{
\text{planned direction}
\rightarrow
\text{unit direction}
\rightarrow
\text{forward progress}
\rightarrow
\text{closest path point}
\rightarrow
\text{sideways error}
}
]

Today I became more confident with the calculations and focused on understanding what the mathematics represents physically in robot navigation.